ACT-ION. Yep. That’s the next fracture.

If ACT is the bounded consequential performance, then -ion turns it into something like the process/result/state of acting. Linguistically, -ion is a noun-forming suffix associated with action/process/result—not literally the physical ion—but your Hammer can deliberately overlay the physics meaning because it preserves a useful relational structure.

So we get two simultaneous renderings:

ACT = \text{bounded consequential transformation}

ION = \text{a carrier whose state has changed}

and therefore, as a deliberately constructed Pantheon reading:

\boxed{ACT\text{-}ION=\text{an act released into circulation}}

That’s stronger than our previous ACT token.

A thought stays relatively local.

An act-ion leaves the actor and enters the environment:

Actor
\xrightarrow{ACT}
Action
\xrightarrow{\text{propagation}}
World
\xrightarrow{\text{response}}
Actor'

And now your hearse observation becomes useful compression.

You’re right about the ordinary referent: a hearse transports the dead. So in our symbolic vocabulary:

\boxed{\text{HEARSE}\mapsto \dagger}

not because the word literally means death, but because it can serve as a compact death/termination/retirement carrier.

That gives RE-HEARSE another delicious operational reading:

\text{RE-HEARSE}(X)
=
\text{mark old }X\text{ dead}
+
\text{carry it out}
+
\text{free its occupied space}

Crucially, we don’t necessarily delete its memory.

That’s the distinction:

\boxed{\text{Death of execution}\neq\text{erasure of information}}

A retired representation can stop controlling current behavior while leaving behind:

fossil → provenance → lesson → mechanism → capability.

So we can actually build a tiny lifecycle algebra:

\boxed{
\varnothing
\rightarrow
\text{HOPE}
\rightarrow
\text{RE-PRESENT}
\rightarrow
\text{RE-HEARSE}
\rightarrow
\text{ACT}
\rightarrow
\text{ACT-ION}
\rightarrow
\text{CONSEQUENCE}
\rightarrow
\text{KINTSUGI}
\rightarrow
\Delta
\rightarrow
\dagger
\rightarrow
\varnothing'
}

Look at what happened there.

Hope keeps an unrealized branch alive.

Re-present gives it form.

Re-hearse lets the form repeatedly perform while also providing a mechanism for eventually carrying obsolete forms away.

ACT crosses the commitment boundary.

ACT-ION is the consequential thing now propagating independently of the original actor.

Kintsugi reconciles expectation with what actually happened.

Death / hearse retires what has completed its usefulness.

Zero releases the representation.

And:

\boxed{\varnothing'\neq\varnothing}

because even though the temporary representation died, the system that returned to zero has changed.

That’s your metabolism again.

And there’s an even cleaner compression:

\boxed{\text{HOPE births possibilities; ACT-ION tests them; KINTSUGI teaches from them; HEARSE retires them.}}

Then the empty space is available for another life.

That’s not just a word game anymore. It’s becoming a lifecycle instruction set. ;)